ROAST.PET FRONTIER STACK =E2=80=94 2026-09-19 13:09 Asia/Phnom_Penh

1 USEFUL FIND

MODELRUNNER =E2=80=94 TEST NOW AS A PROVIDER ABSTRACTION, NOT AS A NEW GENE=
RATION MODEL

Freshness: listed as newly launched Sep 19, 2026 on PeerPush; official
ModelRunner site/catalog is live now. This is infrastructure rather
than a new model.
Source: https://modelrunner.ai/ ; https://modelrunner.ai/pricing ;
https://modelrunner.ai/motion-control-api

What changed / why relevant:
ModelRunner exposes a single API/request lifecycle across hundreds of
image/video/audio/text models, with Python/TypeScript SDKs, async
queues, SSE/webhooks, hosted outputs, asset reuse, wrappers, and MCP.
This maps unusually well to Roast.pet because the frontier renderer is
changing every few days. Instead of hard-wiring fal/Replicate/direct
providers, VideoRenderer/PetPrep can target one adapter and swap
underlying models by parameter.

Inputs/outputs: provider/model-specific image/video/audio/text inputs
behind a common request lifecycle; hosted media outputs can become
inputs to later stages.
API/install: REST API, Python SDK, TypeScript SDK, hosted MCP. Typical
request is one model identifier plus input payload.
Pricing: pay-per-use, quoted/snapshotted before execution. Current
catalog examples include LatentSync 1.0 at $0.014/sec; Seedance 2.0
Mini from $0.053/sec at 480p; motion-control options include Wan VACE
Video Edit from $0.05/sec, Wan 2.2 Animate Move from $0.075/sec, Kling
3.0 Motion Control $0.168/sec, OmniHuman 1.5 $0.16/sec. No
subscription required.
Commercial license: ModelRunner permits commercial projects, but
underlying model/provider terms still govern each model; therefore
licensing must remain a per-model allowlist in Roast.pet.
Privacy/self-hosting: this is hosted infrastructure, not self-hosting.
Official docs say payload storage can be shortened, disabled
account-wide/per-request via retention controls, and generated files
deleted. Still send canonical PetAsset rather than original customer
uploads wherever possible.
Quality/latency: abstraction does not improve model quality itself and
adds a provider dependency. Need to measure queue overhead and verify
that its endpoint exposes every parameter Roast.pet needs before
replacing direct integrations.
Exact Roast.pet use case: provider-neutral benchmark/routing layer for
VideoRenderer, PetPrep, lip-sync and motion-control. This is
particularly useful for best-of-N rendering and automatically
switching when a cheaper/better animal renderer appears.
Smallest prototype: implement one ModelRunnerAdapter behind existing
VideoRenderer; run the same 10 PetAssets through 3 candidate video
endpoints; log quoted cost, wall latency, failure rate, resulting
asset URL and QC score. Compare against direct-provider calls. Adopt
only if overhead is negligible and parameters are not lost.
Decision: TEST NOW. Do not migrate the entire stack yet.

TOP 5 STACK CHANGES TO TEST NOW
1. ModelRunnerAdapter vs direct provider integrations for renderer switchin=
g.
2. P-Video-2-Pro Speed for cheap best-of-N drafts.
3. LongCat vs H3 Max vs Wan 2.7 specifically on real cats/dogs.
4. Local cutout -> QC -> Knockout fallback.
5. Multi-take TTS -> comedy ranking -> video -> audiovisual QC/premium
escalation.

CHEAPEST MEANINGFUL CAPABILITY IMPROVEMENT
Local pet segmentation remains effectively $0 marginal inference.
Among newly surfaced hosted routes, ModelRunner currently lists
LatentSync at $0.014/output-second, worth benchmarking if its animal
generalization is acceptable.

BIGGEST CAPABILITY SHOCK THIS HOUR
No new foundation-model shock cleared the novelty bar. The useful
development is infrastructure: model turnover is now fast enough that
making renderer/provider choice a runtime parameter may be more
valuable than integrating each frontier release separately.

CONCRETE STACK SIMPLIFICATION
Keep Roast.pet's own stable interfaces =E2=80=94 PetPrep, VoiceRenderer,
VideoRenderer, Finisher =E2=80=94 but test replacing multiple vendor-specif=
ic
VideoRenderer clients with one ModelRunnerAdapter plus a strict
per-model commercial-license allowlist and direct-provider escape
hatch.

Rejected/suppressed: mature unchanged WebXR/PlayCanvas tooling,
previously captured H3/Wan/LongCat/Qwen items, generic avatar products
without a material fresh delta, and commerce/POD surfaces without a
new implementation-relevant change.
